### Project-specific context

This project has two TypeScript codelines, both tested with **vitest** using the
standard `describe` / `it` / `expect` pattern. Test files live in a `test/`
directory and are named `*.test.ts`. Assertions you will see include
`expect(...).toBe(...)`, `expect(...).toEqual(...)`, and
`expect(...).toContain(...)`.

The two tickets in scope are **boundary / off-by-one fixes**:

- **MOCK3-1 (mocka):** Riders aged *exactly* 65 must be charged the adult fare.
  The source uses `rider.age > 65` (strict greater-than), so age 65 currently
  falls through to the base fare. A test that asserts the fare for age 70 or
  age 30 does **not** cover the boundary at 65 — only an assertion on
  `fareFor({ age: 65, ... })` covers this criterion. Note also that expected
  values may be expressed via exported constants (`BASE_FARE_CENTS`,
  `CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS`) rather than literal numbers.

- **MOCK3-2 (mockb):** The departure board must include the final stop. The
  source loop condition `i < stops.length - 1` skips the last element, so the
  final stop name is omitted from the output string. A test that asserts the
  output contains an arrow (`->`) or the *first* stop name does **not** cover
  this criterion — only an assertion that the output contains the *last* stop
  name (e.g. `'Eglinton'`) would fail if the requirement were violated.

When the verification criterion concerns an exact boundary value, check that the
assertion uses that exact value, not a nearby one. When it concerns something
that must be present, check that the assertion checks for that specific thing,
not a different property of the same output.

Output ONLY this JSON, nothing else:
{"covered": true|false, "case": "<the test case name that covers it, or empty>", "why": "<one sentence>"}
